export const DATA_36: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-043", num: 43, title: "Đề 043", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Name-Based Discrimination in Job Applications", paragraphs: ["When people apply for a job, they typically expect to be judged on their qualifications, experience, and skills. However, research conducted over the past two decades has consistently shown that a factor far more basic — a person's name — can significantly influence whether they are invited for an interview. This phenomenon, known as name-based discrimination, has been documented in hiring processes across North America, Europe, and Australia, raising serious questions about fairness and equality in the workplace.", "The most widely cited studies in this field use what researchers call an \"audit\" or \"correspondence\" method. Scientists send out pairs of identical job applications — same qualifications, same work experience, same cover letter structure — to the same employers, with the only difference being the name at the top of the résumé. Applicants with names that sound distinctly white or Western-European tend to receive significantly more interview callbacks than those with names perceived as belonging to ethnic minorities, even when all other credentials are equal. One landmark American study found that applicants with typically white-sounding names received about 50 percent more callbacks than those with Black-sounding names.", "Why does this happen? Researchers have proposed several explanations. Some employers may hold conscious biases and deliberately prefer candidates from certain ethnic or racial backgrounds. More commonly, however, discrimination is thought to operate unconsciously. Hiring managers process large volumes of applications quickly, and names can trigger automatic mental associations — assumptions about a candidate's cultural background, communication style, or even work ethic — before a single line of the résumé has been read. This type of bias is particularly insidious because those who exhibit it are often unaware that they are doing so.", "The effects of name-based discrimination extend beyond the individual. When qualified candidates are routinely overlooked because of their names, organisations lose access to talent they would otherwise benefit from. Companies that rely on biased filtering mechanisms may inadvertently build less diverse workforces, which research has consistently linked to weaker innovation and narrower problem-solving approaches. Societies as a whole also bear the cost: when ethnic minorities are systematically blocked from opportunities due to their names, income inequality widens and social mobility decreases.", "Some people have responded to this reality in a deeply personal way. A growing number of job seekers with non-Western names have reported anglicising their names — that is, adopting a simplified or Western-sounding version — when submitting applications. While this strategy sometimes leads to more interview invitations, it raises profound ethical questions. Should individuals be required to obscure their identity in order to compete fairly in the labour market? Critics argue that placing the burden of adaptation on the discriminated party legitimises the bias rather than challenging it.", "Employers and governments have begun taking steps to address the problem. Structured interviewing protocols, standardised scoring systems, and unconscious bias training have all been introduced in various organisations. One of the most promising approaches is anonymised or \"blind\" recruitment, in which identifying information — including names, photographs, and educational institutions — is removed from applications before hiring managers review them. Several large public-sector employers in France, the United Kingdom, and Australia have piloted blind recruitment schemes, with encouraging results in terms of increasing diversity at the interview stage.", "Despite these efforts, name-based discrimination remains a persistent challenge. Training programmes designed to reduce unconscious bias have produced mixed results, with some studies suggesting that awareness alone is insufficient to change deeply ingrained behavioural patterns. For meaningful progress to occur, most researchers agree that structural solutions — those embedded in the hiring process itself rather than relying on individual goodwill — are essential. Until such systems are widely adopted, a job applicant's name will continue to carry weight that has nothing to do with their ability to do the job."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Governments have successfully eliminated name-based bias through blind recruitment policies.", "Unconscious bias training is the most effective solution to workplace discrimination.", "Job applicants should change their names to improve their chances of being hired.", "Name-based discrimination is a documented and ongoing obstacle to equal opportunity in hiring."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what makes the audit method used in discrimination research particularly reliable?", options: ["It measures the time hiring managers spend reading each résumé.", "It surveys large numbers of hiring managers about their personal preferences.", "It compares applications that are identical in every respect except the applicant's name.", "It tracks the long-term career progress of ethnic minority employees."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the most common way that name-based discrimination is thought to operate?", options: ["Companies rely on outdated recruitment software that filters out foreign-sounding names.", "Hiring managers deliberately skip applications from ethnic minorities.", "Names activate automatic mental associations in the minds of recruiters without their awareness.", "Employers openly favour candidates from their own cultural background."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the automatic mental association triggered by a name", "the type of bias described earlier in the paragraph", "the résumé that hiring managers are reviewing", "the cultural background assumed from an applicant's name"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"anglicising\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["removing a surname entirely from a job application", "translating a name into multiple languages for broader appeal", "legally registering a new name with government authorities", "adapting a name to sound more Western or English in style"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does blind recruitment attempt to reduce name-based discrimination?", options: ["By having two separate panels review each application independently", "By removing names and other identifying details before hiring managers see the applications", "By requiring all candidates to submit applications under a shared anonymous code", "By training recruiters to ignore names and focus solely on work experience"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a measure introduced to tackle name-based discrimination?", options: ["Anonymised recruitment processes that remove identifying information", "Unconscious bias training programmes", "Legal penalties for employers found guilty of discriminatory hiring", "Standardised scoring systems for evaluating candidates"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"insidious\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["widely recognised and easy to measure", "temporary and likely to disappear without intervention", "openly hostile and aggressive in its effects", "harmful in a subtle way that is difficult to detect"], answer: 3 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 6 and 7, what can be inferred about unconscious bias training programmes?", options: ["They have been proven ineffective in every major study conducted on the subject.", "They may raise awareness but are unlikely to be sufficient on their own to produce lasting change.", "They are considered by most researchers to be the single most effective tool against hiring discrimination.", "They have been largely abandoned by organisations in favour of blind recruitment schemes."], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward the current state of efforts to address name-based discrimination?", options: ["Pessimistic — the author concludes that discrimination based on names can never be eliminated.", "Critically concerned — the author acknowledges progress but argues that structural change is still urgently needed.", "Optimistic — the author believes recent policies have largely solved the problem.", "Indifferent — the author presents facts without expressing any particular concern."], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The Culinary Culture of Hanoi", paragraphs: ["Few cities in Southeast Asia can claim a culinary identity as deeply layered and historically rooted as Hanoi, the capital of Vietnam. The food of this ancient city is not merely sustenance; it is a living archive of the city's past, shaped by dynastic courts, colonial encounters, migratory movements, and the ingenuity of generations of street vendors. To eat in Hanoi is, in many ways, to read its history.", "At the heart of Hanoian cuisine lies a philosophy of balance that distinguishes it sharply from the cooking traditions of Vietnam's south. Where Ho Chi Minh City's food tends toward sweetness and generous seasoning, Hanoi's culinary aesthetic prizes subtlety and restraint. Broths are simmered for hours to achieve depth without heaviness. Fresh herbs are used not as garnish but as essential structural elements of a dish, added at the table to allow diners to calibrate flavour to personal preference. This emphasis on nuance reflects the influence of Hanoi's status as the seat of royal courts and administrative power for much of Vietnam's history — a context in which refined taste was considered a mark of civilisation.", "The most iconic of all Hanoian dishes is phở, a noodle soup that has become synonymous with Vietnamese cuisine internationally. Though phở is now eaten throughout Vietnam and across the world, food historians largely agree that its origins lie in the north, specifically in the villages near Hanoi in the early twentieth century. The dish is believed to have emerged from a combination of local Vietnamese beef-cooking traditions and the influence of French colonial culinary practices, which introduced the use of beef bones in stock-making — an ingredient previously little used in Vietnamese cooking. Over time, phở evolved into a dish of remarkable complexity: a clear, golden broth seasoned with star anise, cinnamon, and charred ginger, served with thin rice noodles, slices of beef, and a selection of fresh herbs.", "Alongside phở, bún chả holds a place of particular affection among Hanoians. Unlike phở, which is consumed at any hour of the day, bún chả is traditionally a lunchtime dish — a cultural norm so entrenched that many bún chả vendors close their stalls by early afternoon, their daily supply exhausted. The dish consists of grilled pork patties and slices of belly pork served over a bowl of diluted fish sauce sweetened with sugar and enlivened with garlic, chilli, and lime. White rice noodles and an abundant pile of fresh herbs accompany the broth, and diners assemble each mouthful according to their own taste. The dish gained international attention in 2016 when former United States President Barack Obama dined on bún chả at a modest Hanoi restaurant during an official visit, an event that generated considerable global media coverage.", "Street food culture is inseparable from the identity of Hanoi. The city's pavements and narrow lanes — particularly in the historic Old Quarter — are animated from dawn until late evening by vendors selling everything from bánh cuốn (steamed rice rolls filled with minced pork and wood-ear mushrooms) to cháo (rice porridge served with a variety of toppings). These vendors frequently specialise in a single dish or a narrow repertoire, having perfected their craft over years or decades. The concentration of food stalls within specific streets — a practice sometimes called \"street specialisation\" — means that certain lanes are associated with particular dishes, allowing residents and visitors alike to navigate the city's food geography with remarkable precision.", "The role of the French colonial period, which lasted from the late nineteenth century until 1954, left an enduring mark on Hanoian food that is visible to this day. The bánh mì baguette, café sua da (iced coffee with condensed milk), and the practice of using butter and pâté are all legacies of French culinary influence, absorbed and transformed into something distinctly Vietnamese. Rather than simply adopting French food wholesale, Hanoians adapted these elements to local tastes and available ingredients, producing hybrid forms that are neither purely French nor purely Vietnamese but carry the character of both traditions.", "Today, Hanoi's culinary landscape faces new pressures. The rapid growth of the city, rising rents in the Old Quarter, and the increasing prevalence of international fast food chains have raised concerns among food writers and cultural preservationists about the future of traditional street food culture. Some vendors, priced out of their traditional locations, have moved to less prominent sites or ceased trading altogether. Yet Hanoian food culture has demonstrated remarkable resilience across centuries of change, and many observers remain cautiously optimistic that its most distinctive flavours and practices will endure — carried forward by the city's deep and abiding sense of culinary pride."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Phở and bún chả are the two most important dishes in Vietnamese cuisine.", "Street food vendors in Hanoi are under threat and may disappear in the coming decades.", "French colonialism was the dominant force in shaping modern Hanoian cuisine.", "Hanoi's food culture is a rich, historically shaped tradition that continues to adapt and endure."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"emphasis\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a spoken instruction given by a chef to kitchen staff", "a formal written explanation of a culinary rule", "a strong focus or priority placed on a particular quality", "a decorative element added to improve the appearance of a dish"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what role did French colonial influence play in the development of phở?", options: ["French culinary practices brought the use of beef bones for making broth into Vietnamese cooking.", "French authorities banned the use of pork in northern Vietnamese cooking.", "French chefs introduced rice noodles as a substitute for French pasta.", "French colonists taught Hanoian cooks how to season food with star anise and cinnamon."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is distinctive about the way bún chả is traditionally consumed in Hanoi?", options: ["It is prepared differently in each district of Hanoi, resulting in regional variations.", "It is served only during national festivals and special occasions.", "It is typically shared among a group of diners rather than served as an individual portion.", "It is customarily eaten at midday, with most vendors closing before afternoon ends."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"entrenched\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["so deeply fixed in practice that it is difficult to change", "widely misunderstood by visitors unfamiliar with local customs", "recently established through official government policy", "gradually fading due to changes in modern urban lifestyles"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does \"street specialisation\" allow people in Hanoi to do?", options: ["Find specific foods by navigating to streets associated with particular dishes", "Compare prices across different vendors selling the same type of food", "Learn to cook traditional dishes by watching vendors in the Old Quarter", "Identify which vendors have the most experience based on the length of their street"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following best describes how Hanoians responded to French culinary influences?", options: ["They incorporated French elements but transformed them to suit local tastes and ingredients.", "They rejected French food entirely and preserved only traditional Vietnamese recipes.", "They adopted French dishes without modification and served them alongside local food.", "They used French cooking techniques exclusively while keeping original Vietnamese flavours."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"introduced\" in paragraph 3 refers to an action carried out by _______.", options: ["Hanoian cooks who experimented with combining local and foreign flavour profiles", "French colonial culinary practices that brought new ingredients to Vietnamese cooking", "Vietnamese street vendors who adapted local recipes for colonial customers", "food historians who documented the origins of phở in the early twentieth century"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a feature of Hanoi's street food culture?", options: ["Certain streets in the city are associated with specific types of food.", "Vendors typically focus on preparing one dish or a very limited selection.", "Street food activity takes place from early morning through to late evening.", "Vendors in the Old Quarter are required to obtain a special licence before trading."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 6 and 7, what can be inferred about the long-term resilience of Hanoian food culture?", options: ["It is likely to weaken as younger generations prefer international fast food over traditional dishes.", "It will only survive if the Old Quarter is preserved as a protected cultural heritage zone.", "It depends entirely on the government's ability to protect street vendors from rising rents.", "Its capacity to absorb and localise outside influences may help it withstand future pressures."], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "A Family Tradition of Performing Arts", paragraphs: ["In many cultures around the world, the performing arts — theatre, dance, music, and storytelling — have been transmitted across generations not through formal institutions but through families. Long before conservatories and drama schools existed, children learned to sing, play instruments, and perform by watching and imitating their parents, aunts, uncles, and grandparents. This model of intergenerational artistic transmission remains very much alive today, shaping the careers of countless performers who trace their first lessons not to a classroom but to a kitchen table, a backyard stage, or a community hall.", "The Ramsey family of Nashville, Tennessee, offers one compelling example. For four generations, the Ramseys have produced professional musicians, with each generation mentoring the next in the traditions of country and bluegrass music. The family patriarch, Earl Ramsey, began performing at county fairs in the 1950s. His children grew up listening to rehearsals and were given their first instruments before they started school. By the time Earl's grandchildren reached adolescence, they were already performing alongside older relatives at regional festivals, absorbing not just technical skills but the subtler dimensions of stagecraft — how to read an audience, when to slow a tempo, how to recover gracefully from a missed note.", "What makes family-based performing arts traditions particularly durable is the density of informal learning they provide. In a formal music or theatre school, a student might receive one or two hours of instruction per week. Within an artistically active family, exposure is near-constant. A child growing up in such an environment hears professional-level discussion of technique and interpretation at the dinner table, observes how adult performers handle the emotional demands of creative work, and gradually internalises standards of excellence long before they are capable of articulating those standards themselves. This immersive quality is difficult to replicate in any institutional setting, however well-resourced.", "Family traditions also serve as custodians of forms that might otherwise vanish. Many specialised performance traditions — particular styles of flamenco dancing, regional operatic dialects, indigenous drumming ceremonies — survive today precisely because they were considered too culturally specific or commercially marginal to attract institutional support. In the absence of schools or government funding, it fell to individual families to carry these forms forward. The Morales family of Seville, for instance, has maintained a specific lineage of cante jondo — the deep, emotionally intense style of flamenco singing — across five generations, refusing to modify its characteristic vocal techniques despite pressure from promoters seeking a more commercial sound.", "[1] Yet family-based transmission is not without its tensions and limitations. The same intimacy that enables deep learning can also create stifling pressure. Children raised in performing families sometimes describe feeling that their artistic identity was decided for them before they had the chance to discover it independently. [2] The expectation that they will carry on a family tradition can weigh heavily, particularly when a child's natural inclinations or talents point toward a different artistic form or vocation entirely. [3] In some documented cases, gifted young performers have abandoned their inherited traditions altogether, seeking artistic lives defined by personal choice rather than family obligation. [4]", "There is also the question of what is gained and lost through selective transmission. When a parent teaches a child, they inevitably pass on not only techniques but also habits, preferences, and blind spots. A family tradition that prizes technical perfection may produce disciplined performers but inadvertently suppress experimentation. Conversely, a tradition that values emotional expressiveness above all else may neglect the importance of structural rigour. What is transmitted is never the whole of an art form but always a particular interpretation of it — one shaped by one family's experiences, values, and limitations.", "Despite these complexities, family-based performing arts traditions continue to occupy a unique and irreplaceable position in cultural life. They provide a form of artistic continuity that institutional education, with its standardised curricula and rotating faculty, cannot fully replicate. They preserve micro-traditions of performance that enrich the broader cultural landscape. And at their best, they offer young performers something that no school can manufacture: the experience of growing up inside an art form, absorbing it as naturally as a first language, and eventually making it their own."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Family-based performing arts traditions are superior to formal music and theatre education in all respects.", "The decline of family performing arts traditions is an urgent cultural problem that governments must address.", "Children who grow up in artistic families are more likely to become professional performers than those who do not.", "Family traditions in the performing arts offer unique and irreplaceable benefits, though they also carry significant tensions."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did the younger Ramseys gain from performing alongside older relatives at regional festivals?", options: ["Formal certificates of musical achievement recognised across the industry", "Opportunities to record original music at professional studios in Nashville", "Only the technical skills required to play country and bluegrass instruments", "Subtler performance skills such as reading audiences and recovering from mistakes"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["formal music and theatre schools with institutional settings", "the standards of excellence developed within the family tradition", "a child growing up in an artistically active family", "adult performers who discuss technique at the dinner table"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why did the Morales family refuse to change the vocal techniques of their cante jondo tradition?", options: ["They were committed to maintaining the integrity of their inherited style despite commercial pressure.", "They were bound by a legal agreement to preserve the tradition without alteration.", "They believed that commercial modifications would cause them to lose their audience in Seville.", "They lacked the technical ability to adapt their techniques to more contemporary musical forms."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what difficulty do some children raised in performing families describe experiencing?", options: ["A lack of access to professional performance venues during their early training", "A shortage of peers their own age with whom to practise and develop creatively", "An inability to learn technical skills because informal teaching was too unstructured", "The feeling that their artistic identity had been chosen for them before they could explore it freely"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what risk does a family tradition that prioritises technical perfection above all else carry?", options: ["It tends to cause conflicts between younger and older members of the family.", "It may produce performers who are unable to connect emotionally with live audiences.", "It may unintentionally discourage experimentation and creative risk-taking.", "It is likely to produce technically poor performers due to overemphasis on emotion."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"its\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["the specific lineage of cante jondo that the Morales family has maintained", "the broader flamenco tradition practised across southern Spain", "the commercial music industry's preference for more accessible performance styles", "the Morales family's decades-long presence in Seville"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"custodians\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["innovators who modernise traditional forms for new audiences", "guardians who preserve something from being lost or changed", "critics who evaluate and document rare performance traditions", "administrators who manage funding for cultural institutions"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"inadvertently\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["permanently, in a way that cannot be reversed", "without intention, as an unplanned side effect", "reluctantly, after considerable internal debate", "deliberately and with full awareness of the consequences"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Some speak of feeling trapped between loyalty to their family and the desire for artistic self-determination.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "The Influence of Social Media Platforms on Public Discourse", paragraphs: ["The emergence of social media platforms as dominant channels of public communication has prompted sustained debate among scholars, policymakers, and citizens about the nature and quality of contemporary public discourse. [1] What was once a conversation conducted primarily through newspapers, broadcast media, and face-to-face deliberation has been fundamentally restructured by platforms whose architecture, algorithms, and economic incentives are optimised not for the promotion of informed civic engagement but for the maximisation of user attention and advertising revenue. [2] Whether this restructuring constitutes a net gain or loss for democratic life remains one of the most consequential and unresolved questions of the present era. [3] The answer, most serious analysts now concede, is unlikely to be straightforwardly positive or negative — but rather deeply contingent on factors that are structural, behavioural, and institutional in character. [4]", "The optimistic case for social media rests on its capacity to democratise access to public voice. Prior to the digital era, the ability to address a wide audience was constrained by gatekeepers — editors, broadcasters, publishers — whose decisions about what merited circulation were inevitably shaped by commercial pressures, ideological commitments, and institutional cultures that systematically marginalised certain voices. Social media has substantially dismantled these gatekeeping structures, enabling individuals from historically underrepresented communities to participate in public debate on terms previously unavailable to them. Movements such as #MeToo and Black Lives Matter drew much of their organisational power from the speed and reach of social media platforms, mobilising mass attention to injustices that traditional media had long underreported or misrepresented.", "Yet the same architectural features that enable democratic participation also generate significant pathologies. The algorithmic curation of content — the process by which platforms decide what users see, based on predicted engagement rather than verified accuracy or civic relevance — has been linked to the proliferation of misinformation, the amplification of emotionally charged and divisive content, and the formation of what researchers term \"filter bubbles\": informational environments in which users are predominantly exposed to views that confirm rather than challenge their existing beliefs. The sociological consequences of widespread filter-bubble formation remain disputed, but the concern that prolonged exposure to algorithmically curated homogeneity may erode the shared informational foundations necessary for democratic deliberation is taken seriously by a growing body of researchers.", "The question of emotional contagion adds a further layer of complexity. Studies conducted on major platforms have demonstrated that content expressing strong negative emotions — outrage, contempt, fear — spreads significantly faster and further than content expressing positive or neutral affect. Platform designers did not necessarily intend this outcome; it emerges as a structural property of engagement-optimised systems in which emotional arousal reliably drives interaction. The implication is troubling: a media environment architected around engagement metrics is one that systematically rewards the most emotionally inflammatory expression, regardless of its accuracy or constructive value. Critics argue that this dynamic has contributed to a measurable coarsening of political discourse, in which the performance of outrage has displaced substantive argumentation as the dominant mode of public communication.", "Regulatory responses to these dynamics have been hesitant and fragmented. In the European Union, the Digital Services Act introduced in 2022 imposes transparency obligations on very large platforms and requires them to assess and mitigate systemic risks — including risks to public discourse and democratic processes. In the United States, by contrast, Section 230 of the Communications Decency Act has historically shielded platforms from liability for user-generated content, limiting the leverage available to regulators seeking to impose content standards. The divergence between these two regulatory philosophies reflects deeper disagreements about the proper balance between free expression and the prevention of demonstrable harms — a tension that is unlikely to be resolved through legal instruments alone.", "Some researchers have argued that the framing of social media as a singular cause of democratic deterioration obscures the more important question of institutional design. Platforms are not monolithic entities; their effects on discourse vary substantially depending on design choices, content moderation policies, and the broader information ecosystems in which they operate. A platform that algorithmically amplifies authoritative, fact-checked journalism will produce different discursive outcomes than one that maximises raw engagement regardless of content quality. The implication, for these scholars, is that the relevant policy question is not whether social media is good or bad for democracy, but rather what specific design and governance choices are most likely to align platform incentives with democratic values.", "Underlying all of these debates is a more fundamental tension between two visions of what public discourse is for. One vision, rooted in deliberative democratic theory, holds that healthy public discourse requires conditions of relative epistemic equality — shared facts, good-faith argument, and the genuine possibility of persuasion. The other, more pluralist vision regards discourse as inherently contestatory: a space in which competing interests and identities struggle for visibility and recognition, with no neutral arbiter of truth. Social media, arguably, has amplified the second vision at the expense of the first — not because its designers held any particular democratic philosophy, but because the economic logic of attention capture selects, with merciless consistency, for conflict over consensus."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central concern of the passage?", options: ["To argue that social media platforms should be banned from operating in democratic societies", "To examine the complex and contested effects of social media on the quality of democratic public discourse", "To demonstrate that the benefits of social media for public discourse outweigh the drawbacks", "To propose a detailed regulatory framework that governments should adopt to control social media platforms"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"democratise\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["to remove all forms of editorial oversight from public communication", "to make something accessible to a much broader range of people", "to ensure that political content receives equal distribution across platforms", "to subject a process to formal democratic voting procedures"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what are \"filter bubbles\" as described in the passage?", options: ["Technical tools used by platforms to block harmful or offensive content from users' feeds", "Informational environments where users are mainly exposed to content that reinforces their existing views", "Online communities formed by users who share similar political beliefs and social backgrounds", "Algorithmic systems designed to verify the accuracy of news content before it is distributed"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"drives\" in paragraph 4 refers to what drives interaction on engagement-optimised platforms?", options: ["The deliberate strategies of content creators seeking to maximise their number of followers", "The advertising revenue that platforms receive when users click on sponsored posts", "The recommendations made by platform algorithms based on a user's previous browsing history", "The emotional arousal generated by strongly negative content such as outrage and fear"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which type of content has been shown to spread most rapidly on major platforms?", options: ["Content that expresses strong negative emotions such as outrage, contempt, or fear", "Content that presents verified facts and expert analysis in an accessible format", "Content that invites users to participate in collaborative problem-solving activities", "Content produced by professional journalists working for established news organisations"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does the regulatory approach of the European Union differ from that of the United States regarding social media platforms?", options: ["The EU relies on voluntary compliance from platforms, while the US enforces mandatory content moderation standards.", "The EU requires platforms to remove all political content, while the US permits its free circulation.", "The EU imposes transparency and risk-assessment obligations, while the US has historically protected platforms from content liability.", "The EU focuses on protecting individual user data, while the US prioritises national security concerns."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do some researchers argue about the framing of social media as a cause of democratic decline?", options: ["It is entirely accurate and supported by every major study conducted on the subject.", "It fails to account for the role that individual users play in spreading misinformation online.", "It overlooks the fact that platform effects depend heavily on design and governance choices.", "It overstates the case and ignores the positive contributions social media makes to civic life."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"proliferation\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["the careful verification of content before it is published to a wide audience", "a rapid and widespread increase in the quantity of something", "the deliberate suppression of accurate information by powerful organisations", "a gradual and difficult-to-detect shift in public attitudes over time"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as part of the optimistic case for social media?", options: ["It has enabled historically marginalised communities to participate in public debate.", "It has improved the overall accuracy of information available to the general public.", "It provided organisational power to movements such as #MeToo and Black Lives Matter.", "It has dismantled gatekeeping structures that previously restricted access to public voice."], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This transformation has unfolded with a speed and scale that institutional and regulatory frameworks have struggled to keep pace with.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-049", num: 49, title: "Đề 049", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Impact of Local Industries on Small Towns", paragraphs: ["Across many parts of the world, small towns owe much of their identity, prosperity, and sense of community to a single dominant local industry. Whether it is fishing along a coastal village, farming across a rural plain, mining in a mountainous region, or textile manufacturing in an inland town, the presence of a central economic activity shapes not only how residents earn their living but also how they spend their time, how they organise their communities, and what they pass down to their children. Understanding the relationship between local industries and small-town life helps explain why such communities thrive when their industries flourish — and why they struggle so deeply when those industries decline.", "The economic benefits of a thriving local industry are the most immediately visible. When a factory, a fishery, or a farm operates successfully, it creates jobs not only for those directly employed within it but also for the many businesses that depend on it indirectly — grocery stores, hardware shops, transport companies, and repair services, among others. Economists refer to this effect as the \"multiplier effect\": each job created in the primary industry generates additional employment and spending across the wider local economy. In towns where a single employer accounts for a large proportion of local jobs, the health of that employer is effectively the health of the entire community.", "Beyond economics, a shared industry often forms the foundation of a town's social and cultural identity. In fishing communities along the coasts of Norway and Portugal, generations of families have passed down the knowledge, tools, and customs associated with life at sea, creating a rich cultural heritage that extends far beyond the docks and boats themselves. In coal-mining towns in the United Kingdom and the United States, a distinctive working-class culture emerged around the shared experience of underground labour — one defined by solidarity, mutual aid, and a deep sense of occupational pride. These cultural bonds can be remarkably resilient, persisting even long after the industry that created them has declined or disappeared.", "The dependence of a small town on a single industry, however, also creates significant vulnerability . When that industry contracts — whether due to falling global demand, the exhaustion of natural resources, technological change, or competition from lower-cost producers elsewhere — the effects on the local community can be severe and long-lasting. The closure of a major employer triggers a cascade of consequences: unemployment rises, local businesses lose customers and close in turn, young people leave in search of opportunities elsewhere, property values fall, and public services become harder to fund as the local tax base shrinks. Communities that once radiated confidence and cohesion can fall into cycles of poverty and population decline that prove extremely difficult to reverse.", "Responses to industrial decline in small towns vary considerably. Some communities have successfully reinvented themselves by attracting new industries or developing tourism based on their natural environment or cultural heritage. The former steel town of Bilbao in Spain, for example, transformed itself into a celebrated centre for contemporary art and architecture following the collapse of its industrial base in the 1980s. Other towns have developed craft industries, artisan food production, or digital economy businesses that provide sustainable livelihoods without dependence on a single large employer. These success stories, however, are not universal, and many small towns that have lost their primary industry continue to struggle with limited resources and a shrinking population.", "Government policy plays an important role in determining whether industrial decline leads to lasting recovery or prolonged hardship. Investment in retraining programmes, infrastructure development, and incentives for new businesses can help communities adapt to economic change more quickly. However, policies designed in distant capitals do not always reflect the specific needs and strengths of individual communities, and top-down approaches to regional development have a mixed record in practice. Many researchers and community advocates argue that the most durable recoveries occur when local residents are actively involved in planning and decision-making, rather than being treated as passive recipients of programmes designed elsewhere.", "The story of local industries and small towns is ultimately a story about the deep connections between work, identity, and community. When those connections are severed suddenly, the damage extends far beyond the economic — it touches people's sense of purpose, belonging, and hope for the future. Supporting small communities through industrial change therefore requires more than economic investment: it requires an understanding of what these places mean to the people who call them home."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Small towns should diversify their economies immediately to avoid ever relying on a single industry.", "Local industries only benefit small towns when they are supported by strong government investment programmes.", "Cultural identity in small towns is more important than economic development for ensuring long-term stability.", "The relationship between a dominant local industry and a small town shapes prosperity, culture, and identity, but also creates deep vulnerability when that industry declines."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the \"multiplier effect\" describe?", options: ["The way in which each job created in a primary industry generates further employment and spending across the broader local economy.", "The rapid increase in property values that occurs when a successful industry attracts new residents to a small town.", "A government strategy that multiplies investment in small towns by matching private funding pound for pound.", "The tendency of large companies to expand into multiple towns at once, creating a regional network of employment."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"remarkably\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["gradually and with considerable effort over time", "quietly, in ways that are difficult for outsiders to notice", "surprisingly or impressively, beyond what might be expected", "temporarily, until new economic conditions emerge"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what values characterised the culture of coal-mining towns in the United Kingdom and the United States?", options: ["Devotion to religious traditions brought from immigrant communities in surrounding regions.", "An emphasis on formal education as the primary route to social and economic advancement.", "Solidarity, mutual aid, and a strong sense of pride in their shared occupational identity.", "A focus on individual entrepreneurship and competition for limited underground resources."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 2 (\"the many businesses that depend on it indirectly\") refers to _______.", options: ["the multiplier effect described by economists", "the wider local economy of the small town", "the transport network connecting the town to regional markets", "the primary industry such as a factory, fishery, or farm"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is mentioned as a consequence of the closure of a major employer in a small town?", options: ["Surrounding towns often benefit from an influx of skilled workers seeking new employment opportunities.", "Local authorities typically increase taxes to compensate for the loss of private sector revenue.", "The government is legally required to provide compensation to all workers who lose their jobs.", "Young people tend to leave the community in search of jobs elsewhere, reducing the local population."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"vulnerability\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a guaranteed path toward economic modernisation and diversification", "a temporary period of adjustment that communities typically recover from quickly", "weakness or openness to serious harm and disruption", "an official classification used by governments to direct regional investment"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how did the city of Bilbao respond to the collapse of its industrial base?", options: ["It reinvented itself as a leading destination for contemporary art and architecture after its industrial decline.", "It relocated its population to nearby larger cities with the support of the national government.", "It established a network of small craft breweries and artisan food producers to replace its heavy industry.", "It partnered with international mining companies to develop new underground resources in the surrounding region."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a consequence of industrial decline in a small town?", options: ["The closure of local businesses that depended on the primary industry.", "A fall in local property values.", "A significant rise in violent crime and public disorder.", "Rising unemployment among local residents."], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraph 6 about top-down government approaches to helping small towns recover from industrial decline?", options: ["They tend to fail entirely because national governments have no genuine interest in the welfare of small communities.", "They work best in coastal communities but are largely ineffective in inland towns with mining or manufacturing histories.", "They are always more effective than locally led initiatives because they have access to greater financial resources.", "They may not fully address local needs, and recovery is more durable when residents are actively involved in planning."], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Rise of Homeschooling in the Modern Era", paragraphs: ["For most of the twentieth century, homeschooling — the practice of educating children at home rather than enrolling them in a conventional school — was a marginal phenomenon associated largely with religious families seeking to align their children's education with specific faith-based values, or with families living in remote areas beyond the practical reach of the formal school system. Today, that picture has changed considerably. Homeschooling has grown rapidly across North America, Western Europe, Australia, and parts of Asia, drawing in families from a wide range of socioeconomic and cultural backgrounds who are motivated by an equally wide range of concerns about the quality, safety, and philosophical direction of mainstream education.", "The most pronounced surge in homeschooling occurred during and after the COVID-19 pandemic. When school buildings closed across the world in 2020, millions of families found themselves de facto homeschoolers overnight, managing their children's learning at home with varying degrees of support from their schools. While many families returned their children to conventional schools as soon as it was possible, a significant proportion chose not to — having discovered, during the extended period of home-based learning, that their children thrived in a more personalised environment, or having lost confidence in the ability of the school system to meet their children's individual needs. In the United States alone, the proportion of school-aged children being homeschooled roughly doubled between 2019 and 2021, rising from approximately 5.4 percent to around 11 percent of the student population.", "The motivations that lead families to choose homeschooling are varied and do not reduce neatly to a single ideological position. Some parents are driven by concerns about school safety — particularly in countries where fear of bullying, school violence, or social pressures related to peer comparison and social media are prominent. Others are motivated by dissatisfaction with standardised curricula, which they believe fail to accommodate children with learning differences, exceptional abilities, or simply different learning styles. A growing number of families, particularly those with the flexibility afforded by remote work, cite a desire for greater family cohesion and lifestyle freedom — the ability to travel, to learn through real-world experiences, and to structure the day around the child's natural rhythms rather than the fixed timetable of an institutional setting.", "Proponents of homeschooling frequently point to academic research suggesting that homeschooled children perform, on average, as well as or better than their school-educated peers on standardised tests and university entrance examinations. [1] They also highlight evidence that homeschooled students often develop stronger self-directed learning habits, higher levels of intrinsic motivation, and greater confidence in pursuing unconventional academic or career paths. [2] Critics, however, question whether the positive outcomes associated with homeschooling reflect the method itself or the fact that families who choose it tend to be more educated, more financially stable, and more invested in their children's learning than average. [3] They also raise concerns about the socialisation of homeschooled children, arguing that the absence of daily interaction with a diverse peer group may limit the development of social skills and exposure to different perspectives. [4]", "Homeschooling advocates push back strongly against the socialisation critique, pointing out that most homeschooled children participate extensively in extracurricular activities, community sports teams, music groups, religious organisations, and cooperative learning networks specifically designed for homeschooling families. These environments, advocates argue, often provide richer and more meaningful social experiences than the age-segregated, highly regimented social environment of a conventional school day. The quality of social interaction, they contend, matters more than the quantity, and homeschooled children who engage regularly with mixed-age groups, adults, and community members develop a different but no less valuable set of social competencies.", "The regulatory landscape for homeschooling varies enormously between countries and even between regions within the same country. In some jurisdictions, homeschooling families are required to register with educational authorities, follow an approved curriculum, and submit their children to periodic assessments to ensure they are meeting national learning standards. In others — including several American states — parents face minimal oversight and are largely free to teach whatever they choose in whatever manner they prefer. This regulatory diversity reflects deep disagreements about the balance between parental rights and the state's interest in ensuring that all children receive a minimum standard of education that prepares them for adult life and civic participation.", "As homeschooling continues to grow and diversify, it is increasingly difficult to speak of it as a single coherent movement with uniform characteristics or outcomes. The homeschooled child of a highly educated family following a rigorous academic programme shares relatively little with the child of a family using homeschooling primarily to avoid compulsory attendance requirements. What remains consistent, however, is the fundamental question that the rise of homeschooling poses to conventional educational systems: whether the standardised, institution-based model of schooling that has dominated for over a century is genuinely the best way to nurture the diverse abilities, interests, and needs of every child."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["An investigation into why homeschooling became popular only during the COVID-19 pandemic and has since declined.", "A warning that homeschooling produces socially isolated children who are unprepared for university and professional life.", "A detailed argument in favour of replacing conventional schools with homeschooling across all educational systems.", "An overview of the growth, motivations, benefits, criticisms, and regulatory challenges surrounding modern homeschooling."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what happened to homeschooling rates in the United States between 2019 and 2021?", options: ["The proportion of homeschooled children approximately doubled, rising from around 5.4 percent to about 11 percent.", "They increased marginally, with homeschooling remaining the choice of fewer than 6 percent of families.", "They remained stable because most families returned their children to conventional schools after the pandemic.", "They declined slightly as government investment in the school system increased significantly during the pandemic."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"pronounced\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["clearly noticeable and more significant than usual", "briefly experienced before quickly returning to previous levels", "officially announced through government or academic channels", "gradual and barely noticeable without close observation"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is mentioned as a reason why some families choose homeschooling?", options: ["Dissatisfaction with the high cost of private schooling in countries with poorly funded public education systems.", "Concerns that conventional schools place excessive emphasis on physical activity at the expense of academic learning.", "A desire for greater family cohesion and the freedom to structure learning around the child's natural rhythms.", "Evidence that homeschooled children consistently earn higher salaries in adulthood than school-educated peers."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 4 (\" They also raise concerns about the socialisation of homeschooled children\") refers to _______.", options: ["critics who question whether positive outcomes are caused by the method itself", "proponents of homeschooling who support the method", "researchers who conducted standardised testing on homeschooled students", "parents who chose homeschooling during the COVID-19 pandemic"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is mentioned as a social activity that homeschooled children commonly participate in?", options: ["Internship programmes organised by local government employment agencies.", "Online-only study groups that replace the need for any face-to-face interaction.", "After-school tutoring sessions run by qualified teachers from conventional schools.", "Community sports teams and cooperative learning networks for homeschooling families."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"regimented\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["strictly organised and controlled according to rigid rules", "creative and flexible in response to children's individual needs", "designed to encourage free expression and independent thinking", "intellectually stimulating and academically demanding"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the regulatory approach to homeschooling in several American states suggest?", options: ["American states have recently begun requiring homeschooled children to pass the same exams as school students.", "All American states require homeschooling families to follow a nationally approved standardised curriculum.", "Some American states provide minimal oversight, leaving parents largely free to decide what and how to teach.", "American states are internationally recognised as having the most rigorous oversight of homeschooling practices."], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Such findings have led some education reformers to argue that schools themselves should incorporate more elements of self-paced, interest-led learning into their curricula.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a reason why families choose homeschooling?", options: ["The appeal of lifestyle freedom, including the ability to travel and learn through real-world experiences.", "A desire for the financial savings that homeschooling offers compared to private school fees.", "Dissatisfaction with standardised curricula that do not accommodate different learning styles.", "Concerns about bullying, school violence, and social pressures linked to peer comparison."], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The History and Geopolitics of Petroleum Energy", paragraphs: ["Few substances have shaped the modern world as profoundly as petroleum. From the moment commercial drilling began in earnest in the mid-nineteenth century — most famously with Edwin Drake's 1859 well in Titusville, Pennsylvania — oil has been at the centre of industrial transformation, economic growth, and geopolitical rivalry. What began as a fuel for lamps and lubricants evolved over the following century into the lifeblood of transportation, manufacturing, and military power, making access to oil reserves one of the defining strategic preoccupations of every major nation.", "The early decades of the oil industry were dominated by private enterprise and fierce competition. In the United States, John D. Rockefeller's Standard Oil Company achieved near-total control of American refining capacity by the 1880s through a combination of aggressive acquisition, preferential railroad rates, and deliberate undercutting of competitors. This consolidation of market power alarmed legislators and the public alike, ultimately leading to the landmark 1911 Supreme Court ruling that broke Standard Oil into thirty-four separate companies. The episode established a template that would recur throughout oil history: the tension between the enormous economic power that control of oil resources confers and the political pressures that such concentrated power tends to generate.", "As petroleum demand grew through the first half of the twentieth century, the centre of gravity of the global oil industry shifted dramatically. The major Western oil companies — known collectively as the \"Seven Sisters\" — had secured long-term concessions across the Middle East, Venezuela, and North Africa, giving them effective control over production and pricing in much of the world. This arrangement began to unravel from the 1950s onward as newly independent nations asserted sovereign control over their natural resources. Iran's nationalisation of the Anglo-Iranian Oil Company in 1951, though reversed by a CIA and MI6-backed coup two years later, signalled a new era of resource nationalism that would culminate in the formation of the Organisation of the Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC) in 1960.", "OPEC's founding represented a fundamental shift in the balance of power within the global oil market. By coordinating production levels among member states, the organisation gave oil-exporting nations a degree of collective leverage over global prices that individual countries could not have achieved alone. [1] This leverage was demonstrated with devastating effect during the 1973 Arab oil embargo, when Arab OPEC members cut production and halted exports to nations that had supported Israel during the Yom Kippur War. [2] The resulting oil shock — which quadrupled global prices within months — triggered recessions across the industrialised world, [3] exposed the profound fragility of Western economies dependent on imported energy, and accelerated investment in energy efficiency and alternative fuels that had previously been considered economically marginal. [4]", "The geopolitical dimensions of oil have continued to generate conflict and instability throughout the late twentieth and early twenty-first centuries. Iraq's invasion of Kuwait in 1990, widely interpreted as partly motivated by Kuwait's overproduction relative to OPEC quotas and its suppression of oil prices, prompted a US-led military coalition that restored Kuwaiti sovereignty and secured regional oil flows. The 2003 invasion of Iraq, whatever the official justifications, was broadly understood in strategic terms as partly related to the desire to reshape the political landscape of a region that sits atop the world's largest proven petroleum reserves. More recently, Russia's reliance on oil and gas revenues to fund state expenditure has made energy exports a central instrument of its foreign policy, creating dependencies across Europe that have proved difficult to dissolve rapidly even in the wake of armed conflict.", "The long-term future of petroleum is, however, increasingly uncertain. The scientific consensus on climate change has placed the continued burning of fossil fuels at the centre of global environmental concern, and a growing number of governments, corporations, and financial institutions have committed to reducing their dependence on oil and gas in the coming decades. Technological developments in renewable energy — particularly the dramatic fall in the cost of solar and wind power — have made low-carbon alternatives increasingly competitive with fossil fuels across a wide range of applications. Whether the transition away from petroleum will occur quickly enough to avert the most severe consequences of climate change, or whether the entrenched economic and geopolitical interests bound up with oil will delay it indefinitely, remains one of the defining policy questions of the twenty-first century.", "What is already clear is that the age of petroleum has been one of the most consequential and contested chapters in human history — one whose full implications, both for the global environment and for the distribution of political power, will take generations to assess fully. The decisions made over the next two to three decades about energy investment, international cooperation, and the pace of decarbonisation will determine not only whether the world avoids the worst effects of climate change but also which nations and interests hold the dominant positions in whatever energy order replaces the one that oil built."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["The environmental consequences of burning petroleum are so severe that the transition to renewable energy is already essentially complete.", "Petroleum has profoundly shaped industrial, economic, and geopolitical history, and its future role remains one of the most contested issues of our time.", "OPEC's formation in 1960 was the single most important turning point in the history of global energy politics.", "Western oil companies have always maintained dominance over global oil production despite the formation of OPEC."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was the outcome of the 1911 United States Supreme Court ruling involving Standard Oil?", options: ["The ruling required Standard Oil to share preferential railroad rates with all of its competitors.", "The court ruled that Standard Oil had operated legally and was permitted to continue its existing business practices.", "Standard Oil was forced to dissolve into thirty-four separate, independent companies.", "Rockefeller was required to return control of oil refineries to the federal government as a public utility."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"consolidation\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a legal agreement between rival companies to fix prices jointly", "the process of combining resources or power to achieve unified control", "a period of financial difficulty caused by excessive borrowing", "the rapid expansion of a business into new geographic markets"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what were two immediate consequences of the 1973 Arab oil embargo?", options: ["Western governments immediately launched large-scale renewable energy programmes that replaced oil within a decade.", "Oil prices fell sharply as Western nations rapidly developed domestic shale oil reserves as an emergency substitute.", "OPEC permanently dissolved its production agreements following disagreements between Arab and non-Arab member states.", "Global oil prices quadrupled within months and recessions broke out across industrialised nations dependent on imported energy."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["OPEC member states coordinating production levels in the 1960s", "the governments of Middle Eastern countries negotiating with Iran", "the major Western oil companies known collectively as the Seven Sisters", "newly independent nations asserting control over their natural resources"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how was the 2003 invasion of Iraq broadly understood in strategic terms?", options: ["As an attempt to establish a permanent Western military presence throughout the entire Middle East.", "As a humanitarian intervention designed to protect the Kurdish population from persecution by the Iraqi government.", "As partly connected to the strategic importance of reshaping a region holding the world's largest proven petroleum reserves.", "As a direct retaliation for Iraq's role in organising the 1973 Arab oil embargo against Western nations."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"fragility\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["the tendency to recover quickly from economic disruptions through domestic innovation", "the long-term sustainability of economies that diversify their energy sources early", "a strategic advantage that oil-importing nations had developed over oil-exporting states", "the quality of being easily damaged, weakened, or disrupted under pressure"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what technological development has made renewable energy increasingly competitive with fossil fuels?", options: ["A series of international agreements mandating that all new power plants use only renewable energy sources.", "The discovery of new offshore wind resources large enough to supply entire continental economies.", "The invention of new battery technologies capable of storing renewable energy for months at a time.", "The dramatic reduction in the cost of solar and wind power over recent years."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a consequence of the 1973 oil embargo?", options: ["Recessions spreading across industrialised economies dependent on imported energy.", "The permanent collapse of OPEC as a result of internal disagreements among member states.", "A sharp rise in global oil prices within a short period of time.", "Increased investment in energy efficiency and alternative fuel sources."], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This structural dependency meant that disruptions to supply — whether caused by conflict, embargo, or political instability — could transmit economic shocks across the entire industrialised world almost instantaneously.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Astrobiology and the Search for Extraterrestrial Life", paragraphs: ["Among the most profound questions that science has yet to answer is whether life exists, or has ever existed, beyond the boundaries of Earth. Astrobiology — the interdisciplinary field that draws on astronomy, biology, chemistry, geology, and planetary science to investigate this question — has undergone a remarkable transformation over the past three decades. Once regarded by mainstream scientists as an essentially speculative pursuit on the margins of serious inquiry, it has [1] emerged as a well-funded, institutionally recognised discipline whose findings have begun to reshape our understanding of what life requires, where it might arise, and how common or rare it may be across the universe. [2] This shift has been driven above all by a cascade of discoveries that have progressively expanded the boundaries of what is considered a habitable environment. [3] Perhaps the most consequential of these was the realisation, beginning in the 1970s and confirmed through subsequent decades of research, that life on Earth is far more tenacious than previously assumed. [4]", "The discovery of extremophiles — organisms capable of surviving and even thriving in conditions once thought universally lethal — fundamentally altered astrobiological thinking. Thermophilic bacteria flourish in the boiling waters of hydrothermal vents on the deep ocean floor, entirely beyond the reach of sunlight and relying instead on chemosynthesis rather than photosynthesis for energy. Psychrophiles metabolise and reproduce in the frozen brines beneath Antarctic ice sheets at temperatures well below zero. Radiation-resistant organisms have been found surviving in environments with radiation levels that would be immediately fatal to most forms of life. Each such discovery widened the envelope of conditions under which biology might operate — and by extension, widened the range of planetary environments that astrobiologists could plausibly consider as potential habitats for life beyond Earth.", "The solar system itself has become a focal point of this expanded thinking. Mars, once dismissed as a barren and geologically dead world, has been revealed by successive generations of orbital and surface missions to have possessed liquid water on its surface for extended periods in its early history, to harbour subsurface water ice at its poles, and possibly to retain liquid water beneath its southern ice cap today. The discovery of recurring slope lineae — dark streaks on Martian hillsides that appear to form and fade seasonally — raised the possibility, still debated, of present-day briny liquid water at or near the surface. Europa, one of Jupiter's moons, and Enceladus, a moon of Saturn, are now considered among the most promising candidates for extraterrestrial life in the solar system, owing to evidence of vast liquid water oceans beneath their icy surfaces, combined with hydrothermal activity at the ocean floor that may provide the chemical energy necessary to support simple organisms.", "Beyond the solar system, the detection of exoplanets — planets orbiting stars other than the Sun — has opened an entirely new dimension to the search for life. Since the first confirmed exoplanet discoveries of the 1990s, astronomers have identified thousands of exoplanets using techniques such as the transit method, in which a planet's passage across the face of its host star causes a measurable dimming of the star's light. A growing subset of these planets occupies what astronomers call the \"habitable zone\" — the range of orbital distances from a star at which liquid water could, in principle, exist on a planetary surface. The James Webb Space Telescope, launched in 2021, has opened new possibilities for atmospheric characterisation of exoplanets, allowing scientists to search for biosignatures — chemical markers such as oxygen, methane, and certain combinations of gases whose simultaneous presence in a planetary atmosphere would be difficult to explain by non-biological processes alone.", "The interpretation of potential biosignatures, however, requires considerable caution. The presence of oxygen in a planetary atmosphere, for example, does not necessarily imply biological activity, since it can be produced by photolysis — the breakdown of water molecules by ultraviolet radiation — without any involvement of life. Methane, similarly, can arise from geological processes such as serpentinisation quite independently of biology. Astrobiologists therefore argue that the detection of any single biosignature must be regarded as suggestive at best; only the simultaneous presence of multiple gases in proportions that defy straightforward abiotic explanation would constitute genuinely compelling evidence of a living biosphere. The challenge of distinguishing genuine biological signals from abiotic mimics is one that researchers grapple with continuously, and it is a challenge that will require increasingly sophisticated observational tools and theoretical frameworks to meet.", "The broader philosophical implications of discovering extraterrestrial life — even in the form of microbial organisms on a neighbouring moon — would be far-reaching. Such a discovery would carry profound consequences for our understanding of life's place in the universe: it would transform biology from the study of a single known example of life into a genuinely comparative science, reveal whether the emergence of life is an inevitable consequence of the right chemical conditions or a vanishingly rare accident, and fundamentally challenge many of the assumptions embedded in human religious, philosophical, and cultural traditions about the uniqueness of life on Earth. Whether or not extraterrestrial life is ultimately found, the methods, technologies, and conceptual frameworks that astrobiology has developed in pursuit of this question have already enriched our understanding of the conditions that make life possible — and of the extraordinary resilience with which life, once established, tends to persist."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central claim of the passage?", options: ["Astrobiology has evolved from a marginal pursuit into a serious discipline, driven by discoveries that expand our understanding of where life might exist and how to detect it.", "The James Webb Space Telescope has already identified several exoplanets with atmospheric biosignatures confirming the presence of extraterrestrial life.", "The discovery of extremophiles proves that life will inevitably evolve on any planet that contains liquid water.", "Astrobiology has conclusively proven that microbial life exists on at least two moons within our solar system."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"tenacious\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["dependent on sunlight as the primary source of energy for all metabolic processes", "highly adaptable to environments with extreme temperature variations", "chemically complex in ways that make it difficult to synthesise artificially", "stubbornly persistent and capable of surviving in very difficult conditions"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do thermophilic bacteria found at deep-ocean hydrothermal vents obtain energy?", options: ["They absorb dissolved minerals through their cell walls using a passive diffusion process unique to deep-sea organisms.", "They form symbiotic relationships with other bacteria that produce energy through photosynthesis near the ocean surface.", "They convert the chemical energy of inorganic compounds through chemosynthesis rather than relying on sunlight.", "They store solar energy during brief seasonal periods when vent activity carries them closer to the water's surface."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The phrase \"these planets\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["the gas giant planets used as reference points for calibrating the transit detection method", "the rocky planets of the inner solar system studied by early planetary missions", "the moons of Jupiter and Saturn identified as candidates for extraterrestrial life", "the thousands of exoplanets that astronomers have identified orbiting stars beyond the Sun"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why are Europa and Enceladus considered promising candidates for extraterrestrial life?", options: ["They have thin but detectable atmospheres containing oxygen and methane in proportions consistent with biological activity.", "Both moons have surfaces warm enough to sustain liquid water during seasonal periods of solar proximity.", "Evidence suggests they both have subsurface liquid water oceans and hydrothermal activity that could supply chemical energy to organisms.", "Robotic landers have retrieved soil samples from both moons showing the presence of complex organic molecules."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"inevitable\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["dependent on the prior existence of specific planetary geological features", "gradually emerging over billions of years as chemical complexity slowly increases", "certain to occur given the right conditions and therefore impossible to prevent", "extremely rare and unlikely given the complex conditions that must align simultaneously"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does the transit method detect exoplanets?", options: ["By analysing chemical changes in stellar spectra that occur when a planet's atmosphere absorbs starlight.", "By observing the slight decrease in a star's brightness when a planet passes across its face.", "By detecting the faint thermal radiation emitted by a planet as it moves away from its host star.", "By measuring subtle gravitational distortions in the light emitted by distant stars caused by orbiting planetary masses."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why must the presence of oxygen in a planetary atmosphere be interpreted with caution as a biosignature?", options: ["Oxygen is too unstable a molecule to persist long enough in a planetary atmosphere to be detected by current telescopes.", "Most known life forms consume rather than produce oxygen, making its atmospheric presence an indicator of sterility.", "Current atmospheric analysis technologies cannot reliably distinguish atmospheric oxygen from oxygen produced by volcanic activity.", "Oxygen can be generated by the photolysis of water molecules through ultraviolet radiation without any biological involvement."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Prior to these discoveries, the implicit assumption in much of biology was that life was a phenomenon tightly constrained to a narrow set of Earthlike conditions.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a potential consequence of discovering extraterrestrial life?", options: ["Many religious, philosophical, and cultural assumptions about Earth's uniqueness would be fundamentally challenged.", "Biology would become a genuinely comparative science with more than one known example of life to study.", "Humanity would immediately begin planning missions to make direct contact with the discovered life forms.", "It would reveal whether the emergence of life is an unavoidable outcome of the right conditions or an extremely rare event."], answer: 2 }] },
  ] },
];
